# The one source of the compiler, language and runtime-ABI versions. Edit
# here and nowhere else.
#
# tools/gen_version_b.sh copies these numbers into src/version.b, which is
# what the compiler itself reads; test/version.sh refuses a stale copy. The
# release packagers read this file to check a release tag matches what the
# tree declares.
#
# This was compiler/version.h, a C++ header, because the stage-0 bootstrap
# was C++ and included it. Nothing compiles it now — every reader parses it —
# so it is a plain file in the format the installed toolchain already ships
# its own VERSION in.
#
# The project is still in the public 1.0 bake, so do not call it 1.0.0 until
# every release gate in ROADMAP.md has passed.
compiler=0.1.44
language=1.0

# 20: beans_json_decode_probe_publish and beans_json_decode_probe — the typed
# JSON decoder's diagnostic probe, moved out of the encoding bridge and made
# per thread. Typed decoding collapses every failure to one error of kind
# "invalid" at the language boundary, so the gate that holds it to the
# JSONTestSuite answer sheet can otherwise pin only accept-or-refuse and never
# why or where; the probe is those four words — status, error code, byte offset
# or record count, field index. The bridge kept them in a file-scope array and
# wrote all four on every call to beans_enc_json_typed_decode_direct, which is
# what json.decode, json.decode_bytes, json.decode_bytes_in_place and
# json.decode_with_options all lower to. That is public API, so two threads
# decoding at once raced the stores — a data race by the language's own memory
# model on the hot path of the framework we benchmark, and the reason TSan
# could not be pointed at any threaded program that decodes JSON (issue #152).
#
# The words leave through the request buffer instead, the way every other
# output of that ABI already travels: req[4], documented "unused" until now,
# carries beans_json_decode_probe_publish, and the bridge calls it once with
# the status it is about to return and the three words it already wrote into
# req[5..7]. That is a compiler-to-runtime contract like ABI 19's three
# widened request layouts, not a call the compiler makes itself — the emitter
# stores the entry's address beside the four callbacks it already stores at
# req[8..11].
#
# The storage had to leave the bridge with it. runtime/encoding must resolve
# against libc alone (test/encoding_symbols.sh), and _Thread_local puts
# __tlv_bootstrap in the object on Darwin, so per-thread words cannot live
# there at all; relaxed 64-bit atomics were the other way and they pull
# __atomic_store_8 on arm32. In the runtime they are ordinary — a
# _Thread_local array with the freestanding fallback reflect_error_code
# already uses, one thread by contract and no TLS runtime service. The bridge
# still names no runtime symbol: it reaches the slot only through the pointer
# in req, exactly as it reaches beans_alloc_bytes through req[9] and the
# encoder reaches beans_bytes_reserve_raw through req[6].
#
# beans_json_decode_probe reads this thread's copy back, and is a hosted row,
# because a Beans program calls it by name and an executable exports no names
# on Linux. It replaces beans_enc_json_decode_probe, which no longer exists:
# the beans_enc_ prefix would be a lie for a function that is not in the
# encoding bridge. Under `beansc run` it answers zeros — typed decoding is not
# lowered in the tree interpreter, so nothing has been filed.
#
# 19: permessage-deflate (RFC 7692) reached std.websocket, and the framing
# bridge grew the one entry the Beans half cannot do for itself.
# beans_ws_valid_utf8 answers the RFC 6455 text check for bytes wslay never
# saw: a compressed message is still compressed when the framer hands it
# over, so its UTF-8 validity can only be decided after the Beans side
# inflates it, and running the bridge's own Table 3-7 walk keeps one
# implementation of that table. Three request layouts widened with it, each
# a compiler-to-runtime contract like ABI 16's order kind rather than a new
# symbol: beans_ws_new takes [2] "permessage-deflate negotiated", which is
# what turns wslay's allowed RSV bits on; beans_ws_queue takes [2] reserved
# bits, so a compressed message can carry RSV1 through
# wslay_event_queue_msg_ex; and beans_zlib_stream_new takes [3] window bits
# (0, or 9..15) for the negotiated LZ77 window. The message event record
# grew the byte that carries those reserved bits out — [u8 opcode][u8 rsv]
# [u64 len][payload], a 10-byte header where it was 9.
#
# 18: beans_contained_enter, beans_contained_leave, beans_contained_caught —
# the runtime half of a `contained` call (spec/CONCURRENCY.md). Containing a
# panic used to need a fiber: the only boundary a failure could stop at was a
# brewed fiber's entry, so a server that wanted a panicking handler to become
# a 500 paid a spawn, two context switches and a join on every request whether
# or not anything ever panicked (issue #145). The unwind that walks the frames
# was never the fiber's — it is the platform's forced unwind, brought in with
# runtime ABI 12 — and it stops wherever a landing pad declines to resume. So
# the boundary moves to a call: the emitter issues the call as an `invoke`
# whose exception edge is a pad that does not resume, and the failure becomes
# a Result at the call site.
#
# The three entries are the count of catch frames standing on one fiber's
# stack, and the pad's read of the report. enter/leave bracket the
# non-failing path; caught hands over the message and takes the fiber back
# out of the unwind. The count is per fiber, not per thread, because a catch
# frame is a frame and one fiber's frames are not on another's stack; enter
# promotes the calling thread to a worker so the count always has a fiber to
# live on, which is what lets a program that never brews contain a panic on
# its main fiber. beans_panic reads the count: a failure unwinds when the
# fiber is a brewed one OR a catch frame stands on it, and the root fiber of
# a plain program with no catch frame keeps today's report and exit 3.
#
# 17: beans_net_send_pair_wait — one write of two buffers, so a response can
# be sent without first being assembled. A server frames a head and already
# holds a body; joining them copies the body, and on a one-megabyte response
# that copy costs more than everything else the process does for that request.
# The entry takes the two buffers and an offset into their concatenation, so a
# short write resumes correctly whether it stopped inside the head or inside
# the body and the caller never has to know which. sendmsg on POSIX — the
# vectored send that still takes MSG_NOSIGNAL, so a peer that has gone away is
# an EPIPE and not a SIGPIPE — and WSASend on Windows. Two buffers rather than
# an iovec array: a response is a head and a
# body, the pair needs no allocation to describe, and an array built per
# response would be the hot-path allocation this entry exists to remove.
#
# 17: beans_rt_host_invoke — the runtime calls its own entries for the tree
# interpreter, so running a program never needs a C compiler. beans_rt_host_
# symbol already answered the address of an entry that lives in this process;
# an address is only usable by the interpreter's 64-bit word ABI, which does
# not cover every hosted shape, and everything wider went to a C shim written
# and compiled with Clang while the program ran. beans_net_send_from_wait is
# four parameters and beans_net_send_pair_wait is six, so under `beansc run`
# every socket write started a compiler — invisible where a toolchain matches
# the host, fatal where none does: the i686 and aarch64 Windows legs died on a
# socket write with "cannot find dllcrt2.o". The entry takes a name, the
# packed words, their count and a result slot, casts each word back to the
# type the named entry really declares — pointers included, which is what
# makes it right where a pointer is half a word — and calls it in-process. It
# answers 1 when it called the entry, 0 when the name is not hosted, and -1
# when the name is hosted but the `extern "C"` declaration naming it does not
# fit, which the interpreter reports against the program rather than re-routing
# to a shim that would call the same function with the wrong words. Every
# hosted row now carries its call beside its address, and beans_rt_host_symbol
# and beans_rt_host_invoke are themselves rows, so an interpreter running under
# an interpreter reaches them the same way at every depth.
# 17: beans_net_send_pair_text — the string form of that vectored send. A
# server holds a response body as a string, the shape a handler hands back,
# and framing it into a Bytes to send it is the copy the pair send exists to
# remove; this entry sends the string body where it already lives, its length
# read with beans_slen exactly as beans_net_send_text reads its text's. It
# reuses beans_net_send_pair_wait's sendmsg engine rather than a second loop,
# driving it from a local req, so it needs no scratch of its own and no
# per-response iovec allocation. std.sock.send_pair_text lowers to it, the way
# std.sock.send_text lowers to beans_net_send_text; the tree interpreter,
# whose bootstrap predates the entry, joins head and body and sends that once,
# which writes the same bytes and short-writes at the same boundary.
# 17 (also): beans_bytes_reserve_raw — the grow hook json.encode_into hands
# the std.encoding.json direct writer so it appends JSON straight into a
# caller-owned Bytes instead of a private malloc it then copies out. One call
# sets the Bytes length and ensures capacity, returning the backing base and
# capacity; the bridge, a separate translation unit that must not touch BList,
# reaches it only through this pointer (req[6]). The Bytes is therefore an
# opaque void* in the entry's own declaration, not a BList*: a call through a
# function pointer whose type differs from the callee's declared type is
# undefined behaviour, so the entry declares the same handle type the bridge's
# typedef does and casts on this side, the way beans_list_new_typed_capacity
# already returns void* rather than BList*. Emitted only into programs that
# call json.encode_into; not reached by name from the interpreter, which runs
# its own append.
# 17 (also): beans_alloc_bytes — the pooled allocator without the recycled-block
# memset, for callers that fill the whole payload the same instant (a decoded
# string's bytes and its NUL terminator, a Bytes buffer's length). The typed
# JSON decoder hands it to its bridge as the string-payload callback so a
# 101 KB /echo body stops zeroing two thousand string buffers it overwrites
# immediately (issue #144). Same size class, header and collector shape as
# beans_alloc; only the recycled payload zeroing is skipped, and only on the
# pooled arm — the non-pooled arm for large blocks still zeroes. It is a row
# in the host table like every other hosted entry, so the interpreter can both
# find its address and call it by word; a row is the only way in, which is what
# keeps an entry from being reachable by address while uncallable by word.
# 16: order kind 7 — a payload-free enum slot is a pointer at its tag word;
# sort, min and max compare the loaded tags, where an older runtime compared
# the pointers. No new symbol: the order kind is a compiler-to-runtime contract
# passed to beans_list_sort/min/max, like ABI 14's beans_list_sort_by_key n=1
# change. slot_cmp now also traps an unknown kind instead of sorting by the raw
# slot, so a compiler/runtime mismatch fails loudly rather than silently.
# 15: std.term's four entry points. beans_term_is_tty, beans_term_size,
# beans_term_set_raw and beans_term_restore are the struct-shaped half of a
# terminal, kept in C because `struct termios` is 72 bytes on macOS and 60 on
# Linux and `struct winsize` has no portable Beans spelling. Each returns a
# plain status — 0, or negative on failure (-errno on POSIX, -1000 where the
# platform has no terminal control) — and a size comes back through the
# caller's buffer, so a status is never read as a count. Compiled only at the
# full runtime profile; the checker refuses std.term below it, so a smaller
# build links none of this.
# 14: `for x in xs` over a List got the invalidation rule maps already had.
# beans_list_iter_invalid is the panic a loop raises when the list it is
# reading was structurally changed under it. BList's last word became two:
# change_count at offset 40 and change_kind at offset 44, which a generated
# loop loads together as one 8-byte word and compares, and which every
# structural list entry point (push, push_typed, insert, insert_typed,
# remove, remove_typed, clear, reverse, sort, sort_by, sort_by_key and their
# decv_/val_ twins) moves. The struct is still 48 bytes, so a list still fits
# the 64-byte allocation class it has always used. beans_list_sort_by_key,
# beans_list_decv_sort_by_key and beans_list_val_sort_by_key now call the key
# function once per item at n = 1 instead of returning above the key loop.
# 13: display width became a measure the language has. beans_str_width
# answers string.width() in columns, and beans_width_utf8 is the same
# measure over a pointer and a length — the tree interpreter calls it so
# that both compilers read one table. The derived rendering of a Map, a
# Result, a struct and a class instance added beans_show_map_iter, which
# pushes a map's entries onto the iterative show driver's stack, and
# beans_show_enter / beans_show_push_leave, which mark the objects on the
# path so a reference cycle prints <cycle> instead of running forever, and
# beans_list_join_wide, which joins a list whose elements are too wide for
# one runtime slot by handing the driver each element's address.
# 12: the contained-panic unwind (issue #44, spec/CONCURRENCY.md). A program
# that brews now emits a fiber-entry cleanup pad that calls
# beans_fiber_unwind_finish to end the fiber once its frames have run their
# defers and dropped what they owned. The runtime gained that entry along with
# beans_fiber_begin_unwind, beans_fiber_unwinding and beans_fiber_message,
# which start the unwind and report it for the double-panic case. Emitted only
# for a program that brews on a target whose unwinder the backend uses; every
# other build keeps the earlier abandon behaviour and links none of it.
# 11: the fiber runtime landed (spec/CONCURRENCY.md) — the beans_fiber_*
# core (spawn, park, yield, resume, cancel, join, worker bootstrap) and the
# brew layer the compiler lowers onto: beans_brew, beans_brew_typed,
# beans_brew_join, beans_brew_value, beans_brew_value_typed,
# beans_brew_message, beans_brew_cancel, beans_brew_scope_join. Channels
# gained the non-blocking twins beans_chan_try_send, beans_chan_try_send_typed,
# beans_chan_try_recv and beans_chan_try_recv_typed.
# Version 10: async/await left the language, and the hidden executor entries
# left the runtime with it — beans_task_slot, beans_set_task_slot and the
# parked-readiness registry (beans_reactor_note_park, beans_reactor_bind_park,
# beans_reactor_forget_park, beans_reactor_stale_park, beans_reactor_park_dead,
# beans_reactor_note_close, beans_reactor_shutdown_parks) are gone.
# Version 9 resolved reflection handles — beans_reflect_method_handle,
# beans_reflect_method_call_handle, beans_reflect_initializer_handle,
# beans_reflect_initializer_call_handle, beans_reflect_function_handle and
# beans_reflect_function_call_handle — so cached descriptors stop
# re-resolving strings on every call.
# Version 8 added the beans_poll_wait_into runtime entry, which fills a
# caller-owned event buffer instead of returning a fresh one.
# Version 7 added Bytes.filled, Bytes.append_int_text, and Thread.detach.
# Version 6 added per-thread runtime-hook guard entry points.
runtime_abi=20
